Chapter 9 — Part 1
Morning light hit the net café windows as Curtis pushed the door open. UA uniform on, tie loose, duffel over one shoulder. He’d slept in the booth again—four hours, maybe five. The chair had left a crease in his back. He yawned wide enough to crack his jaw, then squinted at the street.
“I should really find a proper place after school. Jeez.”
The words came out rough with sleep. He rolled his neck, braids shifting, and started walking toward the station. Commuters moved around him. A couple of girls on the opposite sidewalk slowed when they passed, eyes catching on the height and the uniform, but he didn’t notice. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while he checked the train time and kept moving, already thinking about the first bell and whatever Aizawa had planned for the day.
The U.A. gates were already busy when Curtis reached them. He kept his eyes low, wireless earbuds in, one thumb hooked in the duffel strap. The track in his ears was something low and steady—his own half-finished demo from a few nights ago. He didn’t look up at the usual morning clusters.
Mina spotted him first from the path. She elbowed Uraraka, grinning. “There he is. Still in the uniform. Bet he didn’t even go home.”
Uraraka’s cheeks went pink on sight. She waved, small. Curtis didn’t catch it; the earbuds stayed in. He nodded once at the gate guard out of habit and kept walking, boots even on the pavement.
Iida’s engines idled nearby as he directed foot traffic. “Good morning, Beardy! Earphones during transit are acceptable, but please remain aware of your surroundings on campus!”
Curtis tugged one bud free, voice still rough with sleep. “Morning. I see the path.”
He put it back in and continued toward the main building. A few more heads turned—Jiro’s earjacks twitching at the faint leak of music, Momo glancing over with quiet interest, Bakugo scoffing from further ahead and deliberately not looking. Curtis didn’t speed up or slow down. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed exactly where it always was while the gates fell behind him and the first bell waited up the hill.
Curtis popped both earbuds out and slowed just enough to fall in step with Iida. A small smile tugged at his mouth.
“You know, your quirk and your whole look remind me of another hero I saw on TV.”
Iida’s engines gave a short, pleased rev. He adjusted his glasses, posture straightening even more. “Ah! You must be referring to my elder brother, the Turbo Hero: Ingenium! He is a remarkable pro and the reason I chose this path. Many people note the family resemblance in both ability and demeanor.” His hand chopped the air once, formal and bright. “It is an honor that you recognized it, Beardy. Have you followed his work closely?”
“Caught a few of the highway clears,” Curtis said. “Clean. Fast. Didn’t waste movement. Figured the engines ran in the family.”
Iida looked genuinely pleased. “Indeed! I strive to uphold the same standard. If you ever wish for details on his older operations, I have compiled extensive notes. Purely for educational purposes, of course.”
“Might take you up on that.” Curtis slipped one earbud back in, still smiling faintly. “See you in class, Iida.”
“Likewise! Please stay aware of your surroundings!”
Curtis nodded and kept walking. Iida watched him go for a second, engines humming contentedly, before returning to his self-appointed gate duty. The low, steady pull in Curtis’s chest stayed quiet as ever.
Curtis pushed into the classroom and stopped just inside the door.
Aizawa was already there, zipped to the chin in the bright yellow sleeping bag, only his hair and half-lidded eyes visible above the desk. He looked dead to the world. The capture weapon lay coiled beside him like it had given up too.
Curtis huffed a quiet breath. “I should’ve brought mine.”
He crossed the room, dropped his duffel, and sat on the floor right next to the sleeping bag, back against the wall, one knee up. Earbuds still in. Eyes already drifting half-shut like he might actually join the nap.
The class stared.
Mina slapped a hand over her mouth to muffle the laugh. Kirishima grinned wide. Kaminari whispered, “Is he seriously—” before Jiro elbowed him. Uraraka’s cheeks went pink all over again. Even Bakugo paused mid-scowl, thrown off. Midoriya’s pencil hovered over a fresh page, unsure whether this counted as hero analysis.
Iida’s engines sputtered. “B-Beardy! That is highly improper! You cannot simply—Mr. Aizawa is resting—!”
Aizawa didn’t move. One eye cracked open just enough to register the tall shape beside him, then slid shut again. The sleeping bag shifted an inch like he’d decided it wasn’t worth the energy.
Soft chuckles rippled through the room. Mina was already recording. Curtis stayed where he was, shoulder almost brushing the yellow fabric, and let his eyes close the rest of the way while the class tried (and failed) to keep quiet before the bell.
The bell cut through the room. Aizawa shifted inside the sleeping bag, one eye cracking open.
“Beardy. To your desk.”
Curtis gave a lazy thumbs-up, pushed off the floor, and dropped into his seat. He rested his chin on his hands, eyes half-lidded, already looking like he might finish the nap there.
Outside, the morning news played on someone’s phone—All Might officially joining the U.A. faculty. By the time homeroom started, reporters had swarmed the gates. Aizawa dealt with them on the way in, capture weapon snapping once when a particularly bold one tried to follow him through the entrance. The defensive barrier flared, metal shutters slamming down. The reporter yelped. The rest of the press got the message.
Back in class, Aizawa unzipped enough to speak. “You need a class representative. It’s extra work and extra eyes on you. Figure it out. Quietly.”
Nearly every hand shot up. Iida’s engines revved as he stood. “We should decide this properly! A vote!”
They voted. When the tally hit the board, Curtis Beardy sat at the top. Momo Yaoyorozu took deputy by a clear margin. Mina whooped. Uraraka clapped, cheeks pink. Midoriya looked stunned. Bakugo scoffed.
Curtis lifted his head. “I resign.”
The room blinked. He shrugged, practical. “It should go to Midoriya. I have work after school. Can’t stay late for meetings.”
Momo glanced at him, then at Midoriya, already recalculating. Aizawa’s visible eye narrowed, but he didn’t argue. “Fine. Midoriya, you’re up. Yaoyorozu stays deputy unless she objects.”
Midoriya’s mouth opened, closed, then he nodded, small and determined. “I-I’ll do my best.”
Curtis dropped his chin back onto his hands. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet. Outside, the barrier lights still glowed, and the press kept filming the gates while Class 1-A settled into the new arrangement.
Momo turned in her seat, expression careful. “Work after school? That host club… what do your parents think of that?”
The room had gone quieter than usual. A few heads tilted. Curtis didn’t look away from the front of the class. His voice stayed even, accent low.
“I don’t have parents. Grew up alone. Far as I can remember, I never had someone I’d call mom or dad.”
Momo’s eyes softened. She opened her mouth, then closed it, the usual precise reply stalling. Uraraka’s hands had gone still on her desk. Mina’s grin faded into something smaller. Even Iida’s engines idled down.
Curtis shrugged one shoulder, like the fact didn’t need more weight than he’d already given it. “Club pays. Hours work around class. That’s enough.”
Aizawa watched from the sleeping bag, one eye open, but didn’t comment. Momo nodded once, quiet. “I see. If you need anything regarding the schedule, the deputy role can cover it.”
“Appreciate it.”
The low, steady pull in his chest stayed exactly where it always was. Class moved on. The barrier lights outside kept glowing, and no one pressed the subject further.
Aizawa’s visible eye stayed half-lidded, but the thought moved clear behind it.
No parents. Explains the self-reliance. Also explains why he doesn’t flinch at sleeping in a net café or picking up night work. Not looking for pity. Just stating the constraint. I’ll adjust the rep duties around it. Still watching the power ceiling.
Around the room the quiet stretched in different directions.
Momo’s pencil rested against her notes. He said it so plainly. No drama. I should have asked differently.
Uraraka’s fingers curled against her skirt. Alone… that long? And he still showed up, still fought, still smiled at me after the match. I didn’t know.
Mina’s usual bounce had settled. Damn. Okay. No more jokes about him ditching for the club. At least not today.
Midoriya’s notebook stayed closed for once. He resigned so he could work. And he grew up without… I should make sure the class stuff doesn’t pile on him.
Iida’s engines were perfectly still. A difficult circumstance. He carries it without complaint. I will ensure the representative duties do not interfere with his employment.
Jiro’s earjacks lay quiet against her shoulders. Explains the late nights. And the music, maybe.
Bakugo stared at his desk, palms unlit. Tch. So what. Plenty of people deal with worse. Doesn’t change the fight.
Todoroki’s expression didn’t shift. Another one who learned early not to expect anything from home.
The rest of the class sat with the information in their own ways—some sympathetic, some awkward, some already moving on. Curtis kept his chin on his hands, eyes forward, the low steady pull in his chest unchanged while the room slowly found its noise again.
The lunch bell rang. Curtis was already moving. One second he sat at his desk; the next the chair was empty. A faint blur at the door—too quick for most eyes—and he was gone. Blink. The hallway outside stayed clear.
In the dining hall, Midoriya, Uraraka, and Iida claimed a table near the windows. Midoriya poked at his rice, shoulders tight.
“I don’t know if I can do the representative thing. Everyone’s expecting me to have answers and I barely—”
“You will manage,” Iida said, hand chopping once for emphasis. “I voted for you myself. Your intuition during the entrance exam—prioritizing rescue points—showed clear leadership potential. The class sees it too.”
Uraraka smiled, cheeks faintly pink. “Yeah! You’ll be great, Deku. And Momo’s deputy, so you’ve got backup.”
Midoriya’s ears went red. “T-thanks. Both of you.”
Uraraka tilted her head at Iida. “You’re always so formal. Is your family, like… really proper? Or rich?”
Iida hesitated, engines idling. “I… am part of the Iida hero family. My elder brother is the Turbo Hero: Ingenium. I do not usually mention it. People treat you differently once they know.”
Uraraka’s eyes widened. “Ingenium? That’s so cool! No wonder you’re so fast.”
Across the cafeteria, Mina scanned the tables and frowned. “Hey, where’s Curtis? He always grabs food even if he eats it walking.”
Jiro followed her look. “Not here. Didn’t see him leave either.”
Momo glanced toward the doors, thoughtful. “He moved quickly. Perhaps he stepped out.”
Hagakure’s gloves waved. “Maybe he’s on the roof again? Or already heading to that club?”
Uraraka’s attention flicked to the empty spot where Curtis usually sat, the low warmth under her skin noticing the absence more than she wanted to admit. She turned back to the table, smile a little smaller, while the rest of the girls kept glancing at the doors.
Curtis had found a tree on the quieter side of the grounds, back against the trunk, eyes already closed. The shade cut the midday heat. He’d almost managed the start of a nap when footsteps stopped a few meters away.
“You’re not in the cafeteria.”
Shoto’s voice. Even. Curtis cracked one eye. The half-and-half hair and the scar were clear in the light. Shoto stood with his hands in his pockets, expression unreadable.
“Trying to sleep,” Curtis said. Accent low with fatigue. “Cafeteria’s loud.”
Shoto nodded like that tracked. He didn’t leave. “You resigned from representative. Because of work.”
“Yeah.”
“And you don’t have parents.”
Curtis’s eye opened the rest of the way. He studied Shoto for a second, then shut it again. “You eavesdrop or just pay attention?”
“Both.” Shoto’s tone didn’t change. “My father is Endeavor. Everyone knows that. It changes how they speak to me. You said your situation plainly. Most people don’t.”
Curtis was quiet for a moment. The tree bark pressed against his shoulders. “Didn’t see a reason to dress it up. You need something, or you just walking?”
“Walking.” Shoto shifted his weight. “The tree is acceptable. I’ll leave you to the nap.”
He turned and started back toward the main path. Curtis watched him go through one half-lidded eye, then let it close again. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet. The shade held. For a few minutes, at least, no one else came looking.
Curtis’s voice had already gone heavy.
“Don’t let the past or your parents define who you are. Or hold you back. Zzzzzzzzzz…”
Shoto stopped mid-step. He turned.
Curtis’s head rested against the bark, eyes shut, the last of the sentence lost to an even breath. Asleep. Just like that.
Shoto stayed where he was. The words sat in his chest longer than he expected.
Don’t let them define you.
His father’s voice was always louder than that. Endeavor’s expectations. The scar. The ice he’d chosen because it wasn’t his father’s fire. Every time someone looked at him they saw the Number Two Hero’s son first. He’d spent years making sure that was all they got—half of himself, carefully controlled, nothing that could be claimed.
Curtis had said it half-asleep, like it was simple. Like growing up alone and still choosing the shape of his own days was just a fact, not a fight. No speech. No pity. Just the sentence, then snoring.
Shoto’s hand flexed once inside his pocket. The left side stayed cold out of habit. He looked at the sleeping figure under the tree—braids against the bark, uniform rumpled, completely unguarded—and felt the old tightness in his ribs ease by a fraction.
He doesn’t even know what he just said.
Shoto exhaled, quiet. He turned back toward the path, footsteps slower than before. The advice stayed with him anyway, small and stubborn, while Curtis slept on in the shade and the lunch bell’s echo finally faded.
The alarm hit near the end of lunch. Red lights strobed. Students surged for the exits all at once, the hallway outside the dining hall locking into a crush of bodies and noise. Panic moved faster than facts.
Iida was caught in it with the rest until he saw the monitors—reporters, cameras, the barrier already down. No villains. Just press. He looked at Uraraka, engines flaring.
“Uraraka! Make me float—now!”
She activated her quirk on instinct. Iida shot upward, Recipro engines boosting him onto the emergency exit sign above the crowd. His voice cut clean over the chaos.
“EVERYONE, CALM YOURSELVES! IT IS ONLY THE MEDIA! THERE IS NO VILLAIN INTRUSION! PLEASE EXIT IN AN ORDERLY FASHION!”
The stampede broke. Heads turned. The crush eased into something manageable. Uraraka floated back down, cheeks pink, while Iida kept directing from above until the hall cleared.
Across campus, Curtis was already moving. Blink after Blink—hallways, courtyards, the tree line, the gates. A blur in the uniform checking corners, confirming the barrier lights, making sure no one had been trampled in the side paths. He didn’t stop until the alarm cut and the last of the press was sealed outside again.
By the time the class filed back into the room, Midoriya was on his feet.
“I resign as representative.” His voice shook, but it held. “Iida handled the panic better than I could have. He should have it.”
The majority agreed without much argument. Iida’s engines revved in surprise, then settled into a formal bow. “I will not disappoint you.”
Curtis slipped in through the rear door as the vote finished, hair slightly windblown, breathing even. He caught the tail end—Tenya at the front, Momo still deputy, Midoriya looking relieved. Curtis met Iida’s eyes, smiled once, small and genuine, and nodded.
Iida returned it, posture straight. Aizawa watched from the desk, one brow raised at the late arrival, but didn’t call it out. The low, steady pull in Curtis’s chest stayed quiet while the class settled and the new representative took his seat.
Midoriya turned as Curtis dropped into his seat. “Oh, Beardy! Where were you? The alarm went off and—”
Curtis scratched the back of his head, braids shifting, expression easy. “Oh, me? I was sleeping in the yard against a tree. Just woke up.”
Midoriya blinked. “You slept through all that?”
“Guess so.” Curtis shrugged, already resting his chin on one hand again. “Missed the excitement. Iida handled it, though. Looks like.”
Midoriya’s mouth opened, then closed into a small, earnest smile. “Yeah. He did.”
Uraraka glanced over from her desk, cheeks faintly pink, but didn’t add anything. Curtis’s eyes were already half-lidded, the low steady pull in his chest as quiet as ever, while the class moved on around the story he’d just told.
Iida waited until the room had settled into quiet work, then stepped over to Midoriya’s desk, engines low. He kept his voice down.
“Midoriya. A moment.”
Midoriya looked up from his notes. “Yeah?”
Iida adjusted his glasses, expression serious. “I believe Beardy is lying about sleeping through the alarm.” He nodded subtly toward Curtis’s desk. “His shoes. They were clean this morning. Now they are heavily scuffed—soles and sides. That does not happen from napping against a tree.”
Midoriya followed the glance. Curtis had his chin on his hand again, eyes half-closed, but the scuff marks on the boots were visible even from here—fresh, rough, the kind that came from hard stops and fast turns.
“You think he was moving around campus?” Midoriya whispered.
“I do.” Iida’s tone stayed formal, but certain. “He checked the grounds. Then he returned and gave a simpler explanation. I will not press him here. But you should know the representative is aware.”
Midoriya nodded slowly, pencil still. Across the room Curtis didn’t look up. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while Iida returned to his seat and the scuffed shoes stayed exactly where they were.
